Today’s news cycle finds our nation grappling with the heavy questions of accountability and legitimacy—from the Constitutional Court’s ruling on our Auditor General to opposition claims of electoral opacity and the tragic death of a former minister. We also see the human stories of our communities playing out in courts and on our streets, reminding us that the pursuit of justice and order is a daily, often messy, affair.

## Main Stories

1. Auditor General Mwambwa to vacate office after Constitutional Court ruling
   The government has accepted the Constitutional Court’s decision that Dr Ron Mwambwa’s appointment as Auditor General was unconstitutional due to his age, with Attorney General Mulilo Kabesha confirming he will leave office. This raises immediate questions about the vetting process that allowed an ineligible candidate to be appointed in the first place, and we must now watch for who will be nominated next and whether proper due diligence will be applied.

2. Church bodies demand inquest into Mutotwe Kafwaya’s death
   The Council of Churches in Zambia and the Independent Churches of Zambia are pressing for clarity and an official inquest into the death of former Transport minister Mutotwe Kafwaya, following the Inspector General of Police’s disclosure that he was the man fatally wounded in a raid on a house linked to Brain Mundubile. The differing accounts surrounding the incident demand a transparent, independent investigation to establish exactly what happened and to assure the nation that due process is being followed.

3. Luanshya Central MP alleges August polls lacked transparency
   Newly elected Luanshya Central MP Golden Mulenga has stated that the August general elections lacked transparency, claiming the Electoral Commission of Zambia did not follow standard procedure and that stakeholders were not given an opportunity to verify results before they were announced. These are serious allegations against our electoral body that go to the heart of our democratic legitimacy; the ECZ must respond publicly to these specific claims to maintain public confidence in our electoral system.

4. UPND urges Commonwealth and EU to avoid statements that could “endorse insurgency”
   The UPND has called on the Commonwealth and European Union to apply consistent standards when addressing security threats in Zambia, specifically cautioning against statements that could be interpreted as endorsing insurgency or terrorism. The party’s reference to the “alleged militarisation of opposition political parties” is a pointed response to international concerns, and it underscores the need for all parties to submit to the rule of law while the state must ensure its security operations are transparent and lawful.

